                title: "The 6-Step Integration Protocol",
                subtitle: "Root-cause analysis for your mental system",
                emoji: "🛠️",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Steps 1-2: Detect & Expose", body: "Follow this like a root-cause analysis. No meditation required. No incense. Just honest observation and data collection.\n\nSTEP 1 — Daily System Scan (5 minutes, evening)\nThree questions every night:\n• What emotion did I feel but not express today?\n• Where did I catch myself judging someone harshly?\n• What pattern repeated?\n\nWrite it down. Not in your head — on paper or in a notes app. Data you don't record is data you lose.\n\nSTEP 2 — Face the Discomfort (Exposure Protocol)\nWhen resistance hits — tight chest, sudden defensiveness, urge to change the subject or check your phone — that's the shadow waving at you.\n\n1. Pause. Don't react.\n2. Name the feeling + body location: \"Anger in my jaw.\" \"Shame in my chest.\" \"Anxiety in my stomach.\"\n3. Rate intensity 1-10.\n4. Stay with it for 90 seconds without adding a story or suppressing it.\n\nWhy 90 seconds? Neuroscience (Jill Bolte Taylor's research) shows that the chemical lifespan of an emotion in the body is approximately 90 seconds. If it lasts longer, you're re-triggering it with narrative. The feeling itself is brief. Your story about the feeling is what makes it chronic."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Steps 3-4: Dialogue & Refactor", body: "STEP 3 — Active Imagination (Mental Simulation)\nJung's most powerful clinical technique. Think of it as running a controlled thought experiment.\n\n• Sit quietly for 10 minutes.\n• Let an image or \"character\" representing the pattern appear — an angry boss, a scared child, a critical voice, whatever shows up.\n• Ask it questions out loud or in writing: \"What do you need?\" \"What are you protecting?\" \"When did you first appear?\"\n• Record the dialogue.\n\nThis sounds weird. It works. It's the same principle as talking through a bug with a rubber duck — externalizing internal processes makes them visible and debuggable. Many engineers and scientists report breakthroughs in 3-4 sessions.\n\nSTEP 4 — Inner Refactoring\nVisualize the pattern as a faulty circuit or buggy subroutine.\n• See the old code — the automatic reaction, the inherited behavior, the suppressed emotion.\n• Acknowledge what it was trying to do (protect you, keep you safe, get you love).\n• Write the new subroutine: \"I can feel anger AND respond professionally.\" \"I can need help AND be competent.\" \"I can be vulnerable AND be strong.\"\n• Run the new simulation mentally 3 times. Rehearse the updated behavior.\n\nThis is cognitive rehearsal — used in CBT, sports psychology, and military training. It works because your brain can't fully distinguish between vivid mental rehearsal and actual experience. Same neural pathways fire."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Steps 5-6: Express & Observe", body: "STEP 5 — Creative Expression (Externalize the Material)\nGet the shadow content OUT of your head and into the world:\n• Draw the pattern as a system diagram or flowchart.\n• Write unsent \"code comments\" from the shadow part's perspective.\n• Move the feeling through your body: walk fast, box, shake, do pushups, dance badly.\n\nThe point: shadow material that stays internal keeps running as background processes. Externalized shadow material becomes data you can work with.\n\nSurrender = stop forcing the outcome. Let the system show you what it needs. You wouldn't force-quit a process while it's writing to disk. Same principle.\n\nSTEP 6 — Observer Mode (Meta-Cognition Upgrade)\nTrain the executive dashboard:\n\nInstead of \"I'm angry,\" practice \"I notice I'm having the thought that I'm angry.\"\n\nThis single shift creates distance between you and the automatic subroutine. You become the developer watching the code execute rather than the code itself.\n\nResearch (Teasdale et al., MBCT studies) shows 8 weeks of this observer practice reduces rumination by 40-60%. That's not mysticism. That's a controlled trial with measurable outcomes.\n\nThe goal isn't to eliminate the shadow. That's impossible — new experiences keep adding material. The goal is to make the unconscious conscious so it stops running your life from the background.\n\nJung's most famous quote: \"Until you make the unconscious conscious, it will direct your life and you will call it fate.\""),
                ],
                reflectionPrompt: "Which of the 6 steps feels most natural to me? Which feels most uncomfortable? The uncomfortable one is probably where my shadow material is densest."
            ),
            
            // LESSON 4: 30-Day Protocol + Cautions
